**Regulation (EC) No 300/2008 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 11 March 2008 on common rules in the field of civil aviation security and repealing Regulation (EC) No 2320/2002**  

This Regulation establishes common rules to protect civil aviation against acts of unlawful interference that jeopardise the security of civil aviation and provides the basis for a common interpretation of Annex 17 to the Chicago Convention on International Civil Aviation. It is based on the Treaty establishing the European Community, Article 80(2), and is adopted under the procedure of Article 251.  

The Regulation applies to airports serving civil aviation in the territory of Member States, operators providing services at such airports, and entities providing goods and/or services to or through such airports. It also applies to security measures on board aircraft of Community air carriers.  

The primary obligations are:  
- The establishment of common basic standards for aviation security, as detailed in Annex 17, and the setting of common rules for monitoring compliance.  
- The Commission’s authority to adopt general measures amending non-essential elements of the common basic standards by supplementing them, in accordance with the regulatory procedure with scrutiny (Article 19).  
- The Commission’s power to conduct inspections, including unannounced ones, to monitor compliance and make recommendations.  
- Member States’ obligation to draw up and maintain national civil aviation security programmes, airport security programmes, air carrier security programmes, and entity security programmes, and to submit them to the appropriate authority.  
- Member States’ right to apply more stringent measures than the common basic standards, provided they are based on a risk assessment and are proportional, objective, and non-discriminatory (Article 6).  
- Member States’ obligation to inform the Commission of any measures differing from the common basic standards in respect of flights to or over third countries, and the Commission’s authority to draw up responses.  
- Penalties for infringements must be effective, proportionate, and dissuasive (Article 23).  

Key defined terms include:  
- ‘civil aviation’ (Article 3(1));  
- ‘aviation security’ (Article 3(2));  
- ‘operator’ (Article 3(3));  
- ‘air carrier’ (Article 3(4));  
- ‘security restricted area’ (Article 3(13));  
- ‘screening’ (Article 3(8));  
- ‘prohibited articles’ (Article 3(7));  
- ‘in-flight security officer’ (Article 3(30));  
- ‘background check’ (Article 3(15));  
- ‘cabin baggage’ (Article 3(19));  
- ‘hold baggage’ (Article 3(20));  
- ‘potentially disruptive passenger’ (Article 3(18));  
- ‘aircraft security check’ (Article 3(29));  
- ‘aircraft security search’ (Article 3(30));  
- ‘regulated agent’ (Article 3(26));  
- ‘known consignor’ (Article 3(27));  
- ‘account consignor’ (Article 3(28));  
- ‘aircraft security search’ (Article 3(30));  
- ‘security equipment’ (Article 3(12));  
- ‘critical parts of security restricted areas’ (Article 3(14));  
- ‘security costs’ (Article 5);  
- ‘penalties’ (Article 23).  

The Regulation is without prejudice to the application of rules on aviation safety, including the transport of dangerous goods (Article 22).  

It repeals Regulation (EC) No 2320/2002 (Article 23).  

Entry into force: 20 days after publication in the Official Journal of the European Union (Article 24(1)).  

Transitional provisions: Articles 4(2), 4(3), 4(4), 8, 11(2), 15(1) second subparagraph, 17, 19, and 22 apply from the entry into force of this Regulation.  

The Regulation is binding and directly applicable in all Member States.
